window.FIELD_GUIDE = {"stops":[{"id":"honjo","title":"Honjo","ja":"本庄","region":"SAITAMA","label":"The starting line","day":13,"end":14,"color":"#24488c","accent":"#ffca70","crop":"46% 53%","hotel":"Hotel Kan-raku Honjo Waseda Ekimae","address":"2-1-12 Wasedanomori, Honjo, Saitama 367-0030","checkin":"15:00","checkout":"10:00","route":"Arrive in Honjo · collect the car the next morning","drive":"Car pickup: Wed 14 Oct at 09:30","pick":"本庄早稲田駅","type":"stay","sections":{"The area":"<p>Honjo is a useful place to begin this particular trip: somewhere to settle in before collecting the car and heading toward the mountains. The modern Honjo-Waseda station district and the older town around Honjo Station are separate places. Your hotel and Toyota branch are in the former; the old Nakasendo streets are in the latter. That distinction matters when deciding what is actually within an easy stroll of the hotel.</p>","Why it matters":"<p>Honjo was one of the largest post stations on the <strong>Nakasendo</strong>, the inland road connecting Edo and Kyoto. Travellers needed accommodation, food, horses and supplies; the town&#x27;s business grew around that movement. Sections of today&#x27;s streets still contain merchant buildings, storehouses and temples that help explain its former role. <a href=\"https://www.pref.saitama.lg.jp/b0111/midokoroh/midokoro-honjojuku.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Saitama Prefecture&#x27;s Honjo-juku history</a></p><p>Later prosperity came from the silk trade. A particularly tangible survival is the <strong>1896 brick warehouse of the former Honjo Commercial Bank</strong>, built to hold cocoons accepted as collateral. It is an unusually clear example of a local industry becoming architecture: a bank literally needed a large building full of cocoons. <a href=\"https://www.pref.saitama.lg.jp/documents/117103/honjyojyukukai2.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Saitama&#x27;s illustrated heritage guide</a></p>","Three picks":"<ol><li><strong>Honjo-juku and the old Nakasendo streets.</strong> Look for the remaining storehouses and the bank&#x27;s brick warehouse as part of one exploratory walk. Allow about <strong>45–75 minutes</strong>. Search Maps for <strong>旧本庄商業銀行煉瓦倉庫</strong> as a useful starting landmark. The history is in the street and building exteriors as well as any exhibitions.</li><li><strong>Marigold Hill Park.</strong> A modest, easy stop near Honjo-Waseda, with views toward surrounding mountains, including Haruna when conditions cooperate. The autumn flowers are a bonus. Allow <strong>20–40 minutes</strong>, rather than treating it as a major expedition. Maps: <strong>マリーゴールドの丘公園</strong>. <a href=\"https://www.honjo-kanko.jp/sightseeing/leisure/marygold-no-oka-koen/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Honjo tourism</a></li><li><strong>Honjo-Waseda no Mori Museum — conditional on timing.</strong> Local archaeology includes haniwa figures and the smiling shield-bearer behind the city&#x27;s Hanipon mascot. Allow <strong>45–60 minutes</strong>. Maps: <strong>本庄早稲田の杜ミュージアム</strong>. <strong>Its published closure rule makes Tuesday 13 October a closure day:</strong> Monday 12 is a national holiday, so the Monday closure moves to Tuesday. Keep this as a possible return-day option only if there is spare time. <a href=\"https://www.hwmm.jp/introduction/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Museum</a>, <a href=\"https://www.hwmm.jp/outline/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">opening rules</a>, <a href=\"https://www8.cao.go.jp/chosei/shukujitsu/gaiyou.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">2026 holidays</a></li></ol>","A manageable visit":"<p>On arrival, choose the old town or the hill, then eat and settle in. On the 14th, collect the car at <strong>9:30 a.m.</strong> and head inland toward <strong>Hotel Route-Inn Ena</strong>, allowing time for breaks. Recheck this leg using the correct hotel pin. The Toyota pickup and return address is <strong>1-9-41 Wasedanomori, Honjo</strong>. <a href=\"https://rent.toyota.co.jp/sp/shop/detail.aspx?eCode=049&amp;rCode=63401\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Official rental branch</a></p>","Local character":"<p><strong>Hanipon</strong> is Honjo&#x27;s mascot, based on a smiling haniwa figure. <a href=\"https://www.city.honjo.lg.jp/hanipon/profile.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Official profile</a></p>","YOKAI ALERT":"<p><strong>Coverage:</strong> Honjo-Waseda, the old Honjo streets, departure toward Ena, and the final approach from Haruna on 22 October.</p><p><strong>Primary entities: kitsune, nurikabe and baggage-associated tsukumogami.</strong></p><p><strong>At the stop:</strong> kitsune interference takes the form of repeated shopfronts and directions that return the party to the same crossing. Nurikabe obstruction is most significant in narrow lanes between storehouses. A passage that appears shorter on the second attempt is not an authorised shortcut.</p><p><strong>On the route:</strong> the outward and return journeys share a collection point, making false-completion incidents possible. An apparent early arrival at the rental office must be checked against the actual address and date.</p><p><strong>Procedure:</strong> retain the original booking details and keep both travellers on the same route. Do not follow a voice claiming to have moved the rental office. Luggage that begins packing itself is to remain closed until stationary.</p>","NINJA WARNING REPORT":"<p><strong>Risk profile: departure observation and document substitution.</strong> The station district provides access to vehicles and luggage; the older warehouse streets provide concealed approaches. The principal exposure occurs while one traveller handles collection paperwork and the other loads the car.</p><p>On the route, a duplicate itinerary may be inserted at the first rest stop. Keep the rental documents together and compare any proposed route change with the saved plan. A folded crane is not an authorised vehicle-inspection record. Repeat the document check at the final return.</p>","SPECIAL CONCERNS":"<ul><li><strong>Calendar displacement:</strong> pickup is 14 October at 9:30 a.m.; return is 22 October by 6:30 p.m. A second sunrise does not create an additional rental day.</li><li><strong>Key substitution:</strong> retain the issued car key. Acorns, carved seals and miniature swords do not operate the GR Yaris.</li><li><strong>Return-office impersonation:</strong> use 1-9-41 Wasedanomori. Handing the keys to a fox beside a closed shutter does not complete the rental.</li></ul>"}},{"id":"ena","title":"Ena","ja":"恵那","region":"GIFU","label":"The inland crossing","day":14,"end":15,"color":"#135e4a","accent":"#d5ec8b","crop":"30% 68%","hotel":"Hotel Route-Inn Ena","address":"2-7-12 Osashimacho Nakano, Ena, Gifu 509-7205","checkin":"15:00","checkout":"10:00","route":"Honjo → Saku / Okaya → Ena","drive":"Revised leg · driving time to be refreshed","type":"stay","sections":{"The area":"<p>Ena is the first overnight after collecting the car, reached through central Japan instead of going north to Toyama. The aim is a manageable driving day followed by a walk and dinner. Your hotel is <strong>Route-Inn Ena</strong>. Its official page places it about <strong>12 minutes on foot from Ena Station</strong> and <strong>five minutes by car from Ena IC</strong>. <a href=\"https://www.route-inn.co.jp/hotel_list/gifu/index_hotel_id_558/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Official hotel location</a></p><p>The station area and old Oi-juku provide the town outing. Enakyo and Iwamura are separate excursions by car, so choose one only if energy and time allow. The next day continues west through Sekigahara to Maizuru.</p>","Why it matters":"<p><strong>Oi-juku</strong> was the <strong>46th of the Nakasendo&#x27;s 69 post stations</strong>, counting from Edo. The old street layout and surviving buildings give a useful connection to the journeys that sustained the town. This makes Ena a particularly fitting overnight on your own inland crossing. <a href=\"https://www.kankou-ena.jp/446.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Ena tourism&#x27;s Oi-juku guide</a></p><p>The surrounding area also combines older townscapes with industrial history. <strong>Iwamura</strong> preserves a historic castle-town street, while <strong>Oi Dam</strong>, completed in <strong>1924</strong>, transformed the Kiso River landscape at Enakyo through hydroelectric development. <a href=\"https://www.kankou-ena.jp/451.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Iwamura townscape</a>, <a href=\"https://www.kankou-ena.jp/793.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Oi Dam</a></p>","Three picks":"<ol><li><strong>Oi-juku and the Ena town streets.</strong> The easiest choice for an arrival-day wander, with the old Nakasendo as a focus. Allow <strong>45–75 minutes in the district</strong>, plus the walk from the hotel and dinner. Maps: <strong>中山道 大井宿</strong>. <a href=\"https://www.kankou-ena.jp/446.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Local guide</a></li><li><strong>Enakyo and Oi Dam.</strong> A separate scenic outing for water, rock formations and the setting of an early hydroelectric scheme. Allow <strong>45–75 minutes at the chosen viewpoint or walking area</strong>, plus driving. Maps: <strong>恵那峡</strong>. A sightseeing cruise is an optional addition, not part of that time allowance. <a href=\"https://www.kankou-ena.jp/areaguide/enakyo.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Enakyo area guide</a></li><li><strong>Iwamura&#x27;s old castle-town street.</strong> A historical town walk if you want a larger outing beyond central Ena. Allow <strong>60–90 minutes there</strong>, plus driving. Maps: <strong>岩村城下町</strong>. Keep the separate castle-ruins climb optional; it is not included in this walking allowance. <a href=\"https://www.kankou-ena.jp/451.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Ena tourism</a></li></ol>","A manageable visit":"<p>For the easy version, arrive at Route-Inn, walk around the town, eat and sleep. Keep the next morning for <strong>Sekigahara</strong>, rather than filling it with every Ena attraction. The revised inland route includes a substantial section of Route 142 between Saku and Okaya, so it should not be described as uninterrupted expressway driving.</p>","YOKAI ALERT":"<p><strong>Coverage:</strong> Honjo → Saku → Route 142 / Okaya → Ena, with local coverage of Oi-juku, Enakyo and the optional Iwamura excursion.</p><p><strong>Primary entities: tengu, yamabiko and bake-danuki.</strong></p><p><strong>At the stop:</strong> bake-danuki activity centres on accommodation identity, particularly properties with similar addresses. Enakyo adds a water-edge exposure; Iwamura adds wooded approaches. An inn that develops a second entrance while being observed is to be treated as unverified.</p><p><strong>On the route:</strong> tengu interference is concentrated at exposed mountain approaches, while yamabiko can reproduce navigation instructions after the relevant junction has passed. An instruction repeated from the hillside has no priority over the saved route.</p><p><strong>Procedure:</strong> establish the next stop before moving off. Any unexplained navigation change is to be reviewed at a normal parking area. Maintain the confirmed destination: Hotel Route-Inn Ena, 2-7-12 Osashimacho Nakano.</p>","NINJA WARNING REPORT":"<p><strong>Risk profile: pass surveillance and post-town interception.</strong> The Route 142 crossing provides changing sightlines; the old road districts provide alley connections and deep eaves. A ninja may attempt to shorten the party&#x27;s route into an unscheduled mountain excursion.</p><p>Maintain the selected through route and keep the car locked during town walks. Do not pursue a retreating figure into a side valley. At the hotel, luggage bearing an additional clan crest must be reconciled with the original inventory before acceptance.</p>","SPECIAL CONCERNS":"<ul><li><strong>Hotel substitution:</strong> Route-Inn Ena is booked. R9 The Yard is a map-pin error and has no authority to replace it.</li><li><strong>Hostile tanuki:</strong> repeated demands to exchange a suitcase for a sack of leaves indicate that negotiations have ceased to be productive.</li><li><strong>First-day fatigue:</strong> the mountain crossing already consumes attention. Additional gorges, castles and supernatural inspections are optional.</li></ul>"}},{"id":"sekigahara","title":"Sekigahara","ja":"関ヶ原","region":"GIFU","label":"The decisive field","day":15,"end":null,"color":"#842d38","accent":"#ffd994","crop":"22% 50%","hotel":null,"address":"関ヶ原古戦場 決戦地, Sekigahara, Gifu, Japan","route":"Ena → Sekigahara → Maizuru","drive":"Visit on the way · allow 45–90 minutes outdoors","type":"visit","sections":{"YOKAI ALERT":"<p><strong>Coverage:</strong> Ena → Sekigahara, the selected battlefield sites, and departure toward Maizuru on Thursday 15 October.</p><p><strong>Primary entities: hitodama and nurikabe, with possible battlefield apparitions.</strong></p><p><strong>At the stop:</strong> isolated lights at the field margins may move independently of any visible bearer. Apparent gaps between positions can close without a corresponding change in the terrain. The absence of visible combatants does not reduce the administrative significance of the site.</p><p><strong>On the route:</strong> apparition activity may present as banners directing traffic toward a different commander&#x27;s headquarters. Such directions are not part of the Maizuru transfer.</p><p><strong>Procedure:</strong> choose a named visitor destination and use established public paths. Do not follow drifting lights across cultivated land. Count both travellers before leaving; additional members of the party require a current hotel reservation.</p>","NINJA WARNING REPORT":"<p><strong>Risk profile: reconnaissance, false dispatches and recruitment.</strong> The open field provides visibility, but the adjoining woodland and changes in ground level permit concealed observation. The principal ninja objective is to obtain the itinerary or deliver an instruction attributed to a commander.</p><p>Do not accept battlefield messages for onward delivery to Maizuru. No member of the party is authorised to take an oath of service during a sightseeing stop. A messenger arriving from the wrong century is not entitled to use the vehicle&#x27;s charging cable.</p>","SPECIAL CONCERNS":"<ul><li><strong>Unscheduled mobilisation:</strong> being asked to hold a banner may constitute acceptance of a military appointment. Decline the equipment.</li><li><strong>Historical deadline conflict:</strong> the party&#x27;s next overnight is Maizuru on 15 October. Neither army has authority to delay check-in.</li><li><strong>Terrain access:</strong> an empty field remains land with access boundaries. Use the selected battlefield visitor route.</li></ul>","The area":"<p>Leave Ena, visit the battlefield, then continue to Maizuru. The <strong>1600 Battle of Sekigahara</strong> was a decisive step in Tokugawa Ieyasu&#x27;s rise. The interest is in the terrain and the positions of the opposing forces, which gives the apparently empty field its context. Allow <strong>45–90 minutes</strong> for a selected outdoor battlefield walk, with extra time if you choose the memorial museum. Maps: <strong>関ヶ原古戦場 決戦地</strong> for the decisive-battle site, or <strong>岐阜関ケ原古戦場記念館</strong> for the museum. Select the actual destination and parking before relying on a driving total; the shared map currently names Sekigahara town. <a href=\"https://visitgifu.com/see-do/historic-sekigahara-battlegrounds/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Gifu&#x27;s battlefield guide</a>, <a href=\"https://sekigahara.pref.gifu.lg.jp/en/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">official museum</a></p>"}},{"id":"maizuru","title":"Maizuru","ja":"舞鶴","region":"KYOTO","label":"Castle town & naval harbour","day":15,"end":16,"color":"#134f70","accent":"#91e4ed","crop":"85% 70%","hotel":"Hotel Route-Inn Kyoto Maizuru – Nishi Maizuru Ekimae","address":"258-2 Hikitsuchi, Maizuru, Kyoto 624-0841","checkin":"15:00","checkout":"10:00","route":"Ena → Sekigahara → Maizuru","drive":"Revised leg · driving time to be refreshed","type":"stay","sections":{"The area":"<p>The useful distinction here is <strong>west versus east</strong>. Your hotel is in Nishi-Maizuru, near the older castle-town district. The red-brick naval waterfront lies toward Higashi-Maizuru. Think of Maizuru as several areas around a deeply indented harbour; deciding which part to see first saves unnecessary driving back and forth.</p><p>For this trip, Maizuru is a strong stop because it offers substantial history without requiring a large-city sightseeing campaign. A harbour panorama and one historical area would be a good result.</p>","Why it matters":"<p>The older western town grew around <strong>Tanabe Castle</strong>. In <strong>1600</strong>, during the campaign leading to Sekigahara, Hosokawa Yusai defended it against forces aligned with Ishida Mitsunari. The castle site helps connect this apparently quiet port town to the struggle that established Tokugawa rule. <a href=\"https://www.maizuru-kanko.net/en/model/jyokamachi.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Maizuru&#x27;s castle-town guide</a></p><p>A different transformation arrived with the naval base, established in <strong>1901</strong>. The surviving warehouses show how the modern Japanese state built a military port: storage, transport and large quantities of practical brickwork. After the Second World War, Maizuru became a major repatriation port. Its history therefore includes both military expansion and the long, often painful process of people returning home. <a href=\"https://kyugun.jp/en/maizuru.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Naval heritage guide</a>, <a href=\"https://kyugun.jp/en/maizuru/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Maizuru&#x27;s repatriation history</a></p>","Three picks":"<ol><li><strong>Maizuru Red Brick Park.</strong> The obvious introduction to the naval port: former warehouses, harbour surroundings and the scale of the old base. Allow <strong>60–90 minutes</strong>. Maps: <strong>舞鶴赤れんがパーク</strong>. The buildings are interesting as industrial and naval history; there is no need to build the visit around temporary exhibitions. <a href=\"https://maizuru-kanko.net/porttrip/en/audioguide/audioguide01.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Official heritage interpretation</a></li><li><strong>Goro Sky Tower and the viewpoint.</strong> The most useful way to understand the shape of the bay, its coves and the separate town areas. Allow <strong>30–60 minutes at the viewpoint</strong>, plus the approach drive. Maps: <strong>五老スカイタワー</strong>. Choose a clear spell if possible. <a href=\"https://kyugun.jp/en/maizuru.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Naval heritage site&#x27;s viewpoint description</a></li><li><strong>Tanabe Castle site and the western old town.</strong> Convenient for your hotel and a quieter historical counterweight to the naval waterfront. Allow <strong>45–75 minutes</strong>. Maps: <strong>田辺城跡</strong>. Expect a castle site, restored structures and interpretation, rather than an intact original keep. <a href=\"https://www.maizuru-kanko.net/en/model/jyokamachi.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Castle-town route</a></li></ol>","A manageable visit":"<p>Choose the red-brick waterfront on arrival if there is enough afternoon left, then return west for dinner and the hotel. Fit the castle area into an easy walk. Goro is the optional weather-dependent addition. The Repatriation Memorial Museum is a possible substitution for a viewpoint if that history interests you more, rather than a fourth compulsory stop.</p>","YOKAI ALERT":"<p><strong>Coverage:</strong> Sekigahara → Maizuru, Nishi-Maizuru, the red-brick waterfront and the optional Goro viewpoint approach.</p><p><strong>Primary entities: funayurei, umibozu and ittan-momen.</strong></p><p><strong>At the stop:</strong> funayurei exposure is concentrated at the waterline and may begin with a request for a ladle or assistance boarding. A large unlit silhouette rising beyond the harbour is to remain outside the sightseeing programme. Ittan-momen can present as loose white material crossing an otherwise sheltered passage.</p><p><strong>On the route:</strong> wooded approaches and harbour mist may conceal the transition from a road obstruction to a maritime incident. Moving cloth is not a valid direction sign.</p><p><strong>Procedure:</strong> remain in public waterfront areas. Do not approach the edge to improve identification, lend equipment to an unregistered vessel, or follow a white strip of fabric beyond the visitor route.</p>","NINJA WARNING REPORT":"<p><strong>Risk profile: warehouse concealment and lookout substitution.</strong> Brick recesses and the division between the eastern and western town areas create opportunities for the party to be separated. Elevated viewpoints favour long-range observation of the approach road.</p><p>Retain Nishi-Maizuru as the hotel destination even after a waterfront visit. Do not accept instructions to enter a service corridor for a shorter route to the car. A visitor badge that folds into a throwing star is to be left unused.</p>","SPECIAL CONCERNS":"<ul><li><strong>Phantom embarkation:</strong> a harbour visit does not authorise a voyage. Do not board a vessel that is absent from its own reflection.</li><li><strong>Dockside declarations:</strong> only the existing luggage is to travel onward. Sealed crates addressed to an unnamed admiral are outside the baggage allowance.</li><li><strong>Town identity:</strong> the hotel is in Nishi-Maizuru. Reaching the eastern waterfront does not complete the hotel transfer.</li></ul>"}},{"id":"tsuyama","title":"Tsuyama","ja":"津山","region":"OKAYAMA","label":"Stone walls & river legends","day":16,"end":17,"color":"#682f64","accent":"#ffd0b4","crop":"6% 46%","hotel":"Hotel Route-Inn Tsuyama Ekimae","address":"108-1 Yokoyama, Tsuyama, Okayama 708-0862","checkin":"15:00","checkout":"10:00","route":"Maizuru → Tsuyama","drive":"About 2 hr 31 min · before breaks","type":"stay","sections":{"The area":"<p>Tsuyama is an inland castle town in northern Okayama Prefecture. The large castle site, the Yoshii River, old streets and railway heritage give it several distinct things to look at. It is especially well suited to this trip: the drive in is moderate, and the next day&#x27;s circuit visit is nearby in the wider region.</p><p>This is somewhere to arrive with enough afternoon left to wander. The town rewards looking at its layout and buildings, rather than racing from one headline attraction to another.</p>","Why it matters":"<p>After Sekigahara, <strong>Mori Tadamasa</strong> received Mimasaka in <strong>1603</strong> and established the castle that became the domain&#x27;s centre. The complex was an elaborate system of enclosures, walls, gates and towers. Its buildings were dismantled after the Meiji government&#x27;s castle-abolition policy, but the great stone terraces remain; the Bitchu turret has since been reconstructed. The site&#x27;s attraction is how much of the fortress&#x27;s scale you can still read in the landscape. <a href=\"https://tsuyama-machijuu.jp/en/guide/tsuyamajo02/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Tsuyama&#x27;s castle history</a></p><p>To the east, <strong>Joto</strong> preserves part of the town along the <strong>Izumo Kaido</strong>. Merchant houses, narrow plots and bends in the road help show how an Edo-period town worked. There is also a twentieth-century industrial chapter: the surviving locomotive roundhouse dates to <strong>1936</strong>. Together, these places tell a story of administration, road travel and railways occupying the same regional centre. <a href=\"https://tsuyama-machijuu.jp/en/guide/joto/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Joto heritage interpretation</a>, <a href=\"https://www.okayama-japan.jp/en/spot/10791\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">railway museum</a></p>","Three picks":"<ol><li><strong>Tsuyama Castle / Kakuzan Park.</strong> Prioritise the walls, terraces and views over town. Allow <strong>60–90 minutes</strong> and expect stairs. Maps: <strong>津山城 鶴山公園</strong>. This is the clearest introduction to why Tsuyama mattered. October is a visit to the castle landscape; the famous cherry blossom photographs show a different season.</li><li><strong>Joto&#x27;s old streets.</strong> An enjoyable <strong>45–75-minute</strong> walk through the historic district. Maps: <strong>津山市城東伝統的建造物群保存地区</strong>. Look at the fronts of the merchant houses and how the street changes direction. You do not need to enter every preserved residence to enjoy the area.</li><li><strong>Tsuyama Manabi no Tetsudokan.</strong> The roundhouse, turntable and preserved locomotives make this a strong machinery stop. Allow <strong>60–90 minutes</strong>. Maps: <strong>津山まなびの鉄道館</strong>. It is near the station area. The published visitor information lists a <strong>4 p.m. closing time, with last admission at 3:30</strong>, so give it priority on arrival if you want to go inside. <a href=\"https://www.okayama-japan.jp/en/spot/10791\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Official prefectural visitor listing</a></li></ol>","A manageable visit":"<p>Pick <strong>the railway museum plus one outdoor area</strong>, or simply castle plus Joto. Dinner can include <strong>Tsuyama horumon udon</strong>, the local grilled noodle dish with beef offal, if that appeals. Keep Saturday morning comfortable so you can enjoy the circuit without feeling hurried. <a href=\"https://www.japan.travel/en/spot/899/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">JNTO&#x27;s Tsuyama guide</a></p>","Local tradition":"<p>In the Mimasaka / Tsuyama area, a kappa is called a <strong>gongo</strong>. Local library material records the tradition, including stories associated with the Yoshii River. <a href=\"https://crd.ndl.go.jp/reference/entry/index.php?id=1000029843&amp;page=ref_view\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">National Diet Library reference record</a>, <a href=\"https://tsuyamalib.tvt.ne.jp/wordpress/wp-content/uploads/2025/02/201603_ref.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Tsuyama library&#x27;s local legends</a></p>","YOKAI ALERT":"<p><strong>Coverage:</strong> Maizuru → Tsuyama, the Yoshii River crossings, castle grounds, old streets and railway-heritage outing.</p><p><strong>Primary entities: gongo, nekomata and tsukumogami.</strong></p><p><strong>At the stop:</strong> the local river category is gongo. Water-edge challenges and requests to inspect submerged property are to be declined. Nekomata exposure concerns a cat whose apparent tail count changes; do not attempt a manual recount. Unattended historical objects require observation only.</p><p><strong>On the route:</strong> river crossings provide an opportunity for gongo interference with the journey&#x27;s destination. Any request to leave the road and continue along the riverbed is invalid.</p><p><strong>Procedure:</strong> cross by the intended bridge and remain on established visitor paths. No food, luggage or passenger is to be pledged as security in a riverside contest.</p>","NINJA WARNING REPORT":"<p><strong>Risk profile: castle-stair separation and old-street surveillance.</strong> The castle&#x27;s changes in level and the narrow connections between older streets can conceal a second route. The principal risk is one traveller following an apparent shortcut while the other remains on the planned walk.</p><p>Use agreed meeting points at ground level. On arrival and departure, check that both travellers are in the car; a convincing shadow in the passenger seat is insufficient. Do not follow a rooftop signal to an alternative station entrance.</p>","SPECIAL CONCERNS":"<ul><li><strong>Regional terminology:</strong> record river incidents under gongo. Opening a second file under kappa may generate two separate demands for compensation.</li><li><strong>Unauthorised railway movement:</strong> locomotives beginning a conversation remain exhibits. The party is not joining their crew.</li><li><strong>Dinner requisition:</strong> a tanuki claiming control of the horumon-udon supply must not be given the remaining travel budget.</li></ul>"}},{"id":"circuit","title":"Okayama Circuit","ja":"岡山国際","region":"OKAYAMA","label":"Formula One in the hills","day":17,"end":null,"color":"#293645","accent":"#f7bf49","crop":"15% 83%","hotel":null,"address":"岡山国際サーキット, 1210 Takimiya, Mimasaka, Okayama, Japan","route":"Tsuyama → Okayama International Circuit → Himeji","drive":"Visitor programme and access on 17 Oct to be checked","type":"visit","sections":{"The area":"<p>The circuit sits in a rural, hilly part of eastern Okayama Prefecture. This is a motorsport destination rather than a city-centre attraction. Its setting is part of the appeal: an international racing venue appears among wooded slopes and country roads.</p><p>Your map already includes a detour through the circuit area. The next planning step is confirming what visitors can access on <strong>17 October</strong>, since a working circuit&#x27;s arrangements depend on the day&#x27;s activity. The draft does not assume a major race, open paddock, or a particular programme.</p>","Why it matters":"<p>The track opened in <strong>1990 as TI Circuit Aida</strong>. It hosted the Formula One <strong>Pacific Grand Prix in 1994 and 1995</strong>, putting this unlikely location on the world championship calendar. It later adopted the Okayama International Circuit name. Even if nothing major is running during your visit, the place has a genuine F1 connection rather than a vague “racing heritage” sales pitch. <a href=\"https://www.okayama-international-circuit.jp/guide/course.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Circuit&#x27;s official history</a></p><p>The course is <strong>3.703 kilometres</strong> long, with <strong>13 corners</strong>, two principal straights and elevation changes. Corners carry names associated with famous drivers, including Attwood, Redman and Hobbs. The circuit&#x27;s own guide highlights close spectator views and the technical nature of the layout. The interesting question on a visit is how the track actually sits in the terrain: where the braking zones, climbs and drops are, and how much smaller or steeper everything feels than it does on television. <a href=\"https://www.okayama-international-circuit.jp/guide/course.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Official course description</a></p>","Three picks in the circuit area":"<ol><li><strong>The circuit itself.</strong> Watch whatever permitted running is taking place and explore the visitor areas open that day. Allow <strong>90 minutes to 3 hours</strong>, depending on activity and interest. The operator describes practice-day viewing and café visits, but access is event-dependent. <strong>17 October&#x27;s programme and admission remain to be checked.</strong> <a href=\"https://www.okayama-international-circuit.jp/guide/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Circuit visitor guide</a></li><li><strong>Hattoji Furusato Village — optional extra drive.</strong> Thatched farmhouses and a rural plateau landscape offer a quieter companion to the circuit. Allow <strong>45–75 minutes there</strong>, plus the additional journey. Maps: <strong>八塔寺ふるさと村</strong>. It is a separate destination and is not already included in your saved route. <a href=\"https://www.okayama-japan.jp/en/spot/10744\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Okayama tourism</a></li><li><strong>Former Shizutani School — alternative historical detour.</strong> Founded in <strong>1670</strong> by Ikeda Mitsumasa for common people, this is an unusually substantial educational site, with a celebrated lecture hall and enclosed grounds. Allow <strong>60–90 minutes</strong>, plus extra driving. Maps: <strong>旧閑谷学校</strong>. It is another optional addition, not a stop the shared map already contains. <a href=\"https://shizutani.jp/english/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Official history and visitor information</a></li></ol>","A manageable visit":"<p>The intended day is <strong>circuit, lunch, then Himeji for a town walk and dinner</strong>. The village and school are alternatives if the circuit visit is short or access is limited. There is no need to do both. Leave enough afternoon to enjoy Himeji instead of arriving just in time to fall into bed. The retained Ako reservation is a separate accommodation option, not another compulsory stop.</p>","YOKAI ALERT":"<p><strong>Coverage:</strong> Tsuyama → Okayama International Circuit, its public visitor areas, and the optional Hattoji or Shizutani side trips.</p><p><strong>Primary entities: wanyudo, mechanical tsukumogami and nurikabe.</strong></p><p><strong>At the stop:</strong> a burning wheel moving without a vehicle is a wanyudo indicator. Tools and loose components that relocate themselves are to be treated as active equipment regardless of apparent age. Nurikabe may present as a paddock passage terminating where the site plan shows an exit.</p><p><strong>On the route:</strong> the rural approach increases the consequence of taking an unverified turn. A road that returns the car to the same braking marker is not to be repeated for diagnostic purposes.</p><p><strong>Procedure:</strong> remain within authorised visitor areas. Do not attempt to repair, overtake or match the pace of an independently moving wheel. Return to a staffed public area if the route through the site ceases to correspond with posted directions.</p>","NINJA WARNING REPORT":"<p><strong>Risk profile: credential substitution and distraction under engine noise.</strong> Activity at the circuit can conceal movement between vehicles. The objective is access to the rental car or insertion of the party into a programme for which it has no entry.</p><p>Keep the keys with the driver and obtain access directions from the actual venue staff. Do not follow a hand signal from a roof or tree line onto a service road. A pit pass printed on dissolving rice paper is not sufficient evidence of appointment.</p>","SPECIAL CONCERNS":"<ul><li><strong>Unscheduled competition:</strong> the rental Yaris has no race entry in this itinerary. A grid position appearing on the windscreen does not change that status.</li><li><strong>Spectral scrutineering:</strong> retain all existing wheels, seats and passengers. Requests to remove a component as an offering are to be refused.</li><li><strong>Visitor access:</strong> the actual programme and admission arrangements for 17 October remain unconfirmed. The supernatural report does not establish access.</li></ul>"}},{"id":"himeji","title":"Himeji","ja":"姫路","region":"HYOGO","label":"Park, walk, explore","day":17,"end":18,"color":"#235895","accent":"#c4f1f8","crop":"0% 42%","hotel":"Hotel Monterey Himeji","address":"60 Ekimae-cho, Himeji, Hyogo 670-0927","checkin":"15:00","checkout":"11:00","route":"Okayama International Circuit → Himeji","drive":"Revised leg · driving time to be refreshed","type":"stay","notice":"Intended overnight. Shiosai Kirara Shokichi in Ako is also still booked for this night, pending your discussion.","sections":{"The area":"<p>Himeji suits the wish to spend the evening walking around a town. Monterey is directly connected to Himeji Station, putting the station district and the walk toward the castle within reach after checking in. The hotel lists the castle at about <strong>15 minutes on foot</strong>. Treat that as access to the castle area, with additional time for exploring the grounds and interior. <a href=\"https://www.hotelmonterey.co.jp/en/himeji/access/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Hotel access and local guide</a></p>","Why it matters":"<p>The castle is the city&#x27;s historical anchor, recognised as a World Cultural Heritage site. Its fortifications and surrounding grounds give the town walk a clear destination even if another full interior visit does not appeal. Nearby <strong>Koko-en</strong> offers a different type of walking, through a series of traditional garden spaces beside the castle. <a href=\"https://www.hotelmonterey.co.jp/en/himeji/access/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Hotel&#x27;s castle and garden guide</a></p>","Three picks":"<ol><li><strong>The station district and streets toward the castle.</strong> Make this the low-effort arrival outing: walk, choose dinner and return when you feel like it. Allow <strong>an hour or an evening</strong>, depending on food stops.</li><li><strong>Himeji Castle and its surroundings.</strong> An exterior walk works as a short visit; an interior visit needs its own time allowance. Allow <strong>45–75 minutes for an outdoor look</strong>, or <strong>2–3 hours for a fuller visit</strong>. Maps: <strong>姫路城</strong>. Check the actual admission hours before planning entry late in the day.</li><li><strong>Koko-en.</strong> A garden walk beside the castle if you want a quieter morning. Allow <strong>45–75 minutes</strong>. Maps: <strong>好古園</strong>. Its opening hours need to be checked for the final timed itinerary. <a href=\"https://www.hotelmonterey.co.jp/en/himeji/access/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Hotel&#x27;s local guide</a></li></ol>","A manageable visit":"<p>After the circuit, check in and spend the evening walking and eating. Sunday morning can take the castle or garden if desired, followed by the drive to Shin-Osaka. The hotel has parking; its published approach is from the <strong>northbound lane to the entrance on the east side</strong>. <a href=\"https://www.hotelmonterey.co.jp/en/himeji/access/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Official driving directions</a></p>","YOKAI ALERT":"<p><strong>Coverage:</strong> Okayama International Circuit → Hotel Monterey Himeji, the station district, castle approaches and Koko-en option.</p><p><strong>Primary entities: rokurokubi, mokumokuren and nurarihyon.</strong></p><p><strong>At the stop:</strong> upper-window observation beyond normal human reach indicates rokurokubi exposure. Multiple eyes appearing in a paper screen are to be recorded without opening the screen. Nurarihyon infiltration is characterised by an uninvited guest behaving as if the room and refreshments belong to him.</p><p><strong>On the route:</strong> the move from rural roads into the station district creates an opportunity for an additional passenger to claim that he boarded earlier. The authorised party remains two people.</p><p><strong>Procedure:</strong> check the room assignment against the booking, keep keys under control and decline additional occupants. Do not offer tea while a guest&#x27;s right to be present remains unresolved.</p>","NINJA WARNING REPORT":"<p><strong>Risk profile: roofline observation and castle-route diversion.</strong> Castle approaches create strong visual distractions, while the station district provides multiple pedestrian exits. The principal risk is an instruction to meet the other traveller at an unplanned gate.</p><p>Agree on a street-level meeting point and retain it. On the drive into town, continue to the Monterey parking approach rather than a purported castle service entrance. A message delivered through a ventilation grille does not alter the rendezvous.</p>","SPECIAL CONCERNS":"<ul><li><strong>Parallel reservations:</strong> Monterey Himeji and Shiosai Kirara Shokichi are both still booked for 17–18 October. No entity encountered locally may cancel either booking.</li><li><strong>Castle jurisdiction:</strong> a request to take responsibility for defending the western wall is outside the scope of a visitor ticket.</li><li><strong>Parking approach:</strong> follow the hotel&#x27;s published entrance instructions. A gate that opens directly into a seventeenth-century courtyard is not the parking garage.</li></ul>"}},{"id":"osaka","title":"Osaka","ja":"大阪","region":"OSAKA","label":"Two nights. Stay a while.","day":18,"end":20,"color":"#652c79","accent":"#ffbd78","crop":"38% 61%","hotel":"Holiday Inn & Suites Shin Osaka","address":"1-7-31 Nishimiyahara, Yodogawa-ku, Osaka 532-0004","route":"Himeji → Shin-Osaka","drive":"Revised leg · driving time to be refreshed","type":"stay","notice":"Two intentional reservations for the same dates. Full day in Osaka on Monday 19 October.","sections":{"The area":"<p>Osaka is the pause in the middle of the hotel-hopping. You arrive on Sunday, have <strong>all of Monday without changing hotels</strong>, and leave Tuesday. Shin-Osaka is your accommodation base; the principal outings suggested here are elsewhere in the city. Use local transport for a day wandering the centre, and let the car have an unremarkable existence in a parking space.</p><p>Since you have visited Japan many times, there is no need to approach Osaka as a checklist. A historical morning and a long afternoon that turns into dinner would use the stop well. It also gives your companion room to enjoy the atmosphere at his own pace.</p>","Why it matters":"<p>Osaka Castle embodies the city&#x27;s place in the struggle to unify Japan. <strong>Toyotomi Hideyoshi began construction in 1583</strong>; the Toyotomi stronghold fell in <strong>1615</strong>. The Tokugawa rebuilt it, and the main tower seen today dates to <strong>1931</strong>. Its history is therefore layered: successive governments and generations reshaped the same symbolic site. <a href=\"https://www.osaka-info.jp/en/spot/osaka-castle-main-keep/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Osaka&#x27;s official castle guide</a></p><p>The commercial city grew through waterways and entertainment as well as political power. <strong>Dotonbori&#x27;s canal was completed in 1615</strong>, and the surrounding district developed into a theatre and leisure area. <strong>Shinsekai</strong>, developed in <strong>1912</strong>, represents a later moment when Osaka enthusiastically borrowed international ideas about amusement and modern city life. These districts make more sense when seen as successive generations of people going out to enjoy themselves. <a href=\"https://www.suito-osaka.jp/en/info/news/3316/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Dotonbori history</a>, <a href=\"https://osaka-info.jp/en/special/kita-minami/course01/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Shinsekai and Tsutenkaku</a></p>","Three picks":"<ol><li><strong>Osaka Castle grounds and museum.</strong> Allow <strong>90 minutes to 2½ hours</strong>, depending on whether you enter the tower. Maps: <strong>大阪城</strong>. The walls and moats provide an excellent outdoor visit; the museum adds the historical narrative. This is the most substantial history choice of the three.</li><li><strong>Shinsekai and Tsutenkaku.</strong> Walk the streets around the tower and Janjan Yokocho, then choose somewhere for kushikatsu or another meal. Allow <strong>90 minutes to 3 hours</strong>, including food. Maps: <strong>新世界</strong> or <strong>通天閣</strong>. It is a good fit for a sociable afternoon built around the neighbourhood itself. <a href=\"https://osaka-info.jp/en/spot/shinsekai/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Official Shinsekai guide</a></li><li><strong>Dotonbori and the adjoining streets.</strong> A river walk, extravagant signs and a dense concentration of places to eat. Allow <strong>an hour to an entire evening</strong>. Maps: <strong>道頓堀</strong>. Decide whether you want the conspicuous bustle of the main stretch or a quieter meal a few streets away. <a href=\"https://www.suito-osaka.jp/en/history/history_11/history_11_10/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Waterfront history and setting</a></li></ol>","A manageable visit":"<p>Sunday: arrive, settle in and choose dinner. Monday: <strong>one daytime anchor</strong>, then whichever neighbourhood you feel like spending the evening in. There is no need to fit all three picks into one day. Tuesday has a substantial transfer to Fujinomiya, so an easy departure will be more valuable than an additional morning attraction.</p>","Local character":"<p><strong>Billiken</strong>, associated with Tsutenkaku, originated as an American creation before becoming part of Shinsekai&#x27;s identity. <a href=\"https://osaka-info.jp/en/special/kita-minami/course01/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Official account</a></p>","YOKAI ALERT":"<p><strong>Coverage:</strong> Himeji → Shin-Osaka, the possible Ako alternative, the hotel base, Osaka Castle, Shinsekai and Dotonbori outings.</p><p><strong>Primary entities: noppera-bo, nurarihyon and urban tsukumogami.</strong></p><p><strong>At the stop:</strong> a conversation in which the speaker&#x27;s facial features cease to be present indicates noppera-bo exposure. Nurarihyon may occupy a table, order for the group and leave the financial responsibility behind. A vending machine that follows the party between streets is no longer a fixed retail installation.</p><p><strong>On the route:</strong> duplicate exit announcements can redirect the car toward a district already passed. Resolve discrepancies at an ordinary stopping place rather than by circling the same interchange.</p><p><strong>Procedure:</strong> retain the saved hotel destination and use local transport for the planned city outings. Do not pay an account opened by an uninvited guest or follow an appliance into a service passage.</p>","NINJA WARNING REPORT":"<p><strong>Risk profile: distraction, impersonation and separation during evening outings.</strong> Castle grounds favour elevated observation; busy entertainment streets favour the exchange of messages and personal effects. A ninja may present the party with a meeting point that moves whenever it is approached.</p><p>Use a fixed, named rendezvous and keep the hotel address available. On the arrival drive, disregard any instruction to transfer the car to an unbooked safe house. No member of the party is to demonstrate allegiance by leaving a restaurant through the ceiling.</p>","SPECIAL CONCERNS":"<ul><li><strong>Domestic harmony:</strong> no marriage, divorce or clan-affiliation agreement is to be signed between dinner and the last train.</li><li><strong>Tobacco signalling:</strong> an exhalation through the nose does not establish rank. It must not be used to accept responsibility for a district.</li><li><strong>Unclaimed accounts:</strong> a tanuki in a suit remains outside the party of two. His restaurant bill, taxi fare and vending-machine dispute remain his responsibility.</li></ul>"}},{"id":"fujinomiya","title":"Fujinomiya","ja":"富士宮","region":"SHIZUOKA","label":"The mountain & its water","day":20,"end":21,"color":"#283f9e","accent":"#ffb096","crop":"99% 24%","hotel":"Fujinomiya Fujikyu Hotel","address":"15-18 Chuocho, Fujinomiya, Shizuoka 418-0065","checkin":"15:00","checkout":"10:00","route":"Shin-Osaka → Fujinomiya","drive":"About 4 hr 26 min · before breaks","type":"stay","sections":{"The area":"<p>Fujinomiya sits on the southwestern side of Mount Fuji. It works well as a stop where the landscape itself supplies the point: the mountain above the town, clear spring water and excursions toward waterfalls and the plateau. The main shrine is in the urban area; the waterfalls and Lake Tanuki need separate drives.</p><p>There are two sensible versions of this visit. With clear weather, give the mountain views priority. With cloud covering Fuji, enjoy the shrine, water and town, without spending the whole stop chasing a summit that refuses to appear.</p>","Why it matters":"<p>Fujinomiya&#x27;s identity is closely tied to <strong>Fuji worship</strong> and <strong>Fujisan Hongu Sengen Taisha</strong>. The shrine venerates <strong>Konohanasakuyahime</strong>, associated with the sacred mountain. Its tradition connects worship to appeasing Fuji&#x27;s volcanic power, and its history records the move to the present location in <strong>806</strong>. The spring-fed setting is part of that religious landscape. This is a town built around a relationship with the mountain, extending well beyond the modern business of photographing it. <a href=\"https://fuji-hongu.or.jp/sengen/history/index.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Shrine&#x27;s history</a>, <a href=\"https://fuji-hongu.or.jp/sengen/english/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">English introduction</a></p><p>The surrounding water also mattered to pilgrims. <strong>Shiraito Falls</strong> became a place of religious practice associated with Fuji-ko, the movement devoted to Fuji pilgrimage. The waterfall&#x27;s curtain-like form comes largely from groundwater emerging through the volcanic layers. Looking at the scenery with that in mind adds geology and religious history to what would already be a worthwhile view. <a href=\"https://fujinomiya.gr.jp/en/guide/170/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Fujinomiya&#x27;s Shiraito guide</a></p>","Three picks":"<ol><li><strong>Fujisan Hongu Sengen Taisha and Wakutama Pond.</strong> The essential local introduction: shrine buildings, spring water and Fuji&#x27;s religious context. Allow <strong>45–75 minutes</strong>. Maps: <strong>富士山本宮浅間大社</strong>. It is the easiest substantial outing to pair with a night in the town. <a href=\"https://fujinomiya.gr.jp/en/guide/175/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Local tourism guide</a></li><li><strong>Shiraito Falls, with nearby Otodome Falls.</strong> A major scenic outing with a short walking circuit. Allow <strong>60–90 minutes on site</strong>, plus the drive. Maps: <strong>白糸の滝 富士宮</strong>. The descent toward Shiraito&#x27;s basin involves steps; the tourism guide also describes an upper viewing position. These are good subjects even when Fuji itself is obscured. <a href=\"https://fujinomiya.gr.jp/en/guide/170/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Visitor details</a></li><li><strong>Lake Tanuki.</strong> A strong choice for Fuji views and, with cooperative water and weather, its reflection. Allow <strong>45–90 minutes</strong> for a viewpoint and an unhurried walk; a full circuit is optional. Maps: <strong>田貫湖</strong>. The principal attraction is the lake and mountain scenery. <a href=\"https://fujinomiya.gr.jp/en/guide/122/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Official lake guide</a></li></ol>","A manageable visit":"<p>After the Osaka drive, keep Tuesday to <strong>the town, shrine if time permits, and dinner</strong>. Fujinomiya yakisoba is the obvious local food to try. On Wednesday, choose <strong>the falls or Lake Tanuki</strong> before heading to Hashimoto. Doing both may fit, but uses more of the time available for a Sagamihara or Tama outing. <a href=\"https://fujinomiya.gr.jp/en/guides/eat/?term=yakisoba\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Local food listings</a></p>","YOKAI ALERT":"<p><strong>Coverage:</strong> Shin-Osaka → Fujinomiya via the expressway corridor, the town and shrine area, and optional waterfall / Lake Tanuki drives.</p><p><strong>Primary entities: tengu, kitsune and bake-danuki.</strong></p><p><strong>At the stop:</strong> wooded approaches favour tengu interference, while reflections near the water can complicate identification of kitsune and transformed tanuki. Lake Tanuki requires particular attention to the distinction between a public destination and an animal claiming ownership of it.</p><p><strong>On the route:</strong> long expressway transfers provide opportunities for repeated service-area incidents. Encountering the same attendant, with the same untouched cup, after several different junctions requires a location check at the next normal stop.</p><p><strong>Procedure:</strong> keep the town hotel as the arrival objective. Do not enter a forest to recover a sound, follow a reflection onto water, or accept a guarantee that a side road will produce an unobstructed view of Fuji.</p>","NINJA WARNING REPORT":"<p><strong>Risk profile: forest-edge observation and visibility-related diversion.</strong> Waterfall approaches and the wooded Fuji foothills provide cover. The principal objective is to move the party from a short scenic visit into a search of substantially larger terrain.</p><p>No whole-mountain search is authorised. Remain on the chosen visitor route and do not pursue a disappearing figure uphill. During the expressway transfer, do not make an unscheduled exit in response to a banner requesting assistance from the mountain command.</p>","SPECIAL CONCERNS":"<ul><li><strong>View-release charges:</strong> no payment is authorised to an animal, apparition or intermediary claiming to control cloud cover.</li><li><strong>Mountain nomenclature:</strong> referring to Fuji as a hill does not reduce its area, walking distance or administrative requirements.</li><li><strong>Angry tanuki:</strong> attempts to exchange the rental car for a larger vehicle made of leaves are to be declined, regardless of claimed luggage capacity.</li></ul>"}},{"id":"hashimoto","title":"Hashimoto","ja":"橋本","region":"KANAGAWA","label":"Space science & Tama","day":21,"end":22,"color":"#4a387a","accent":"#bcecd9","crop":"47% 66%","hotel":"Toyoko Inn Keio Line Hashimoto Station Kita","address":"3-2-8 Hashimoto, Midori-ku, Sagamihara, Kanagawa 252-0143","checkin":"15:00","checkout":"10:00","route":"Fujinomiya → Hashimoto","drive":"About 1 hr 44 min · before local excursions","type":"stay","sections":{"The area":"<p>Hashimoto is an urban base in Sagamihara, on the edge of the wider Tokyo region. It gives you a straightforward overnight before the final Gunma excursion. The station district supplies the practical evening; the strongest sightseeing choices involve going somewhere nearby with a particular purpose.</p><p><strong>Tama Center is a separate outing</strong>, and so is JAXA&#x27;s Sagamihara campus. Neither is a named stop in your shared driving map. They are included here as choices for the afternoon, with the amount of time left depending on how much you do around Fuji that morning.</p>","Why it matters":"<p>Hashimoto has a road-town history beneath its modern station-centre appearance. It was a stopping point on routes used by <strong>Oyama pilgrims</strong>. Near the Sakai River crossing, travellers could take different ways toward the Sagami River and onward to the mountain. Modern railway development later shifted the area&#x27;s centre toward the station. <a href=\"https://www.city.sagamihara.kanagawa.jp/kankou/1026674/camera/report/1011147/1032668.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Sagamihara&#x27;s pilgrimage-road history</a>, <a href=\"https://www.city.sagamihara.kanagawa.jp/_res/projects/default_project/_project_/00_common/koho/sagamihara20180315.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">city history feature</a></p><p>The wider district offers a striking historical range. <strong>Tsukui Castle</strong> was a Hojo stronghold controlling a strategically important border and transport area; it fell in the campaigns of <strong>1590</strong>. At the other end of the timeline, the space research campus at Sagamihara opened in <strong>1989</strong>. It is possible to spend the same afternoon thinking about a fortified hill and spacecraft. <a href=\"https://www.kanagawa-park.or.jp/tsukuikoshiroyama/history.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Tsukui Castle history</a>, <a href=\"https://www.isas.jaxa.jp/about/facilities/sagamihara.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">JAXA campus history</a></p>","Three picks":"<ol><li><strong>JAXA&#x27;s Communication Hall of Space Science and Exploration.</strong> The strongest technical option for your companion: spacecraft and research interpretation, with large rocket displays outside. Allow <strong>60–90 minutes</strong>. Maps: <strong>JAXA 相模原キャンパス 宇宙科学探査交流棟</strong>. The visitor centre is free; parking is limited. Check its <strong>21 October opening calendar</strong> before building the afternoon around it. This is space science, so choose it according to interest in that subject. <a href=\"https://www.isas.jaxa.jp/en/outreach/visit/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">English visitor guide</a>, <a href=\"https://www.isas.jaxa.jp/visit/guide/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">current visitor information</a></li><li><strong>Tama Center and Sanrio Puroland.</strong> Your previously mentioned Tama outing fits as an optional excursion. A walk around the centre and a visit inside Puroland are very different time commitments: allow <strong>45–90 minutes for an outside wander</strong>, or dedicate several hours if entering the attraction. Maps: <strong>多摩センター</strong> or <strong>サンリオピューロランド</strong>. Check tickets and the operating calendar if choosing the latter. <a href=\"https://www.gotokyo.org/en/destinations/outlying-area/tama/index.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Official Tokyo guide</a></li><li><strong>Tsukuiko Shiroyama Park.</strong> Choose this for the reservoir landscape and the setting of the old Tsukui Castle. Allow <strong>45–90 minutes for an easy park visit</strong>; climbing through the castle site is a more substantial walk. Maps: <strong>津久井湖城山公園</strong>. The castle interest is in its hill, earthworks and strategic position. <a href=\"https://www.kanagawa-park.or.jp/tsukuikoshiroyama/history.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Park&#x27;s historical explanation</a></li></ol>","A manageable visit":"<p>Choose <strong>one afternoon outing</strong>, then settle in near Hashimoto Station. JAXA or Tama would make the day feel purposeful without adding a long evening drive. Thursday already contains the Haruna excursion and car return, so this is a good place to finish reorganising luggage and enjoy a simple dinner.</p>","YOKAI ALERT":"<p><strong>Coverage:</strong> Fujinomiya → Hashimoto, the station district, and optional Tama Center, JAXA or Tsukuiko Shiroyama excursions.</p><p><strong>Primary entities: nurikabe, mokumokuren and equipment-associated tsukumogami.</strong></p><p><strong>At the stop:</strong> nurikabe interference may add a corridor between the station exit and an apparently familiar street. Mokumokuren indicators are to be confined to the affected screen or partition; do not investigate by entering adjoining property. Equipment that moves without its display being activated remains outside the party&#x27;s authority to operate.</p><p><strong>On the route:</strong> city names and station destinations are vulnerable to substitution. Arriving in Tama Center does not place the vehicle at the Hashimoto hotel.</p><p><strong>Procedure:</strong> use Toyoko Inn Keio Line Hashimoto Station Kita as the saved overnight destination. Keep optional outings separate in navigation. If a corridor returns to its own entrance, use a known public exit rather than testing a third circuit.</p>","NINJA WARNING REPORT":"<p><strong>Risk profile: station-exit substitution and access-document forgery.</strong> The boundary between a straightforward hotel transfer and a separate outing creates an opportunity to divide the party. A ninja may claim that the other traveller has moved to a different station or research entrance.</p><p>Keep a common meeting point and verify changes directly with the companion. No visitor is authorised to enter a technical area on the basis of a secret mission. The previously reported absence of ninjas at Tama Center does not extend to adjoining districts or subsequent visits.</p>","SPECIAL CONCERNS":"<ul><li><strong>Launch substitution:</strong> rocket displays are not alternative transport to Honjo. The rental vehicle remains the assigned return method.</li><li><strong>Mascot authority:</strong> possession of a Hello Kitty seal does not permit a third party to amend the car-return deadline.</li><li><strong>Unscheduled excursion:</strong> complete luggage organisation here. Thursday&#x27;s Haruna visit is not to acquire an additional stop through overnight correspondence.</li></ul>"}},{"id":"haruna","title":"Ikaho & Haruna","ja":"伊香保・榛名","region":"GUNMA","label":"The mountain finale","day":22,"end":null,"color":"#135d59","accent":"#c3e7aa","crop":"65% 37%","hotel":null,"address":"榛名湖, Takasaki, Gunma, Japan","route":"Hashimoto → Ikaho / Haruna → Honjo","drive":"Earlier map: about 3 hr 18 min for the whole leg","type":"visit","notice":"Car return: Thursday 22 October by 18:30 in Honjo. Aim for around 17:00 to allow time for fuel and handover.","sections":{"The area":"<p>This is the last scenic excursion of the driving trip: up through the Ikaho area toward the Haruna uplands, then back toward Honjo. There is a real change of setting between the lower town, the climbing road and the volcanic landscape around the lake.</p><p>Your saved map contains a <strong>Haruna detour</strong>, but it does not identify a lakeside car park or ropeway station as a named destination. Add the exact place you decide to visit when finalising navigation; the displayed driving time should not be treated as covering every attraction below.</p>","Why it matters":"<p>Haruna&#x27;s landscape is volcanic. <strong>Lake Haruna lies in a caldera at roughly 1,100 metres above sea level</strong>, with the distinct cone of Haruna-Fuji beside it. That combination of high water, steep-sided mountain and accessible viewpoints explains much of its appeal. The mountain is also the inspiration for <strong>Mount Akina in Initial D</strong>, giving the area a modern motoring association as well as a much older scenic identity. <a href=\"https://www.visit-gunma.jp/en/spots/lake-haruna/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Gunma&#x27;s Lake Haruna guide</a></p><p>Ikaho has a long recorded cultural history, including a mention in the <strong>eighth-century Manyoshu</strong> anthology. Today its centre is arranged around <strong>365 stone steps</strong>, with small businesses and inns along the way and a shrine above. Even without taking a bath, you can enjoy how the town occupies the slope: the stairs are its main street, viewing platform and organising feature. <a href=\"https://www.visit-gunma.jp/en/discover/destinations/ikaho/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Gunma&#x27;s Ikaho history and guide</a></p>","Three picks":"<ol><li><strong>Lake Haruna and a short lakeside walk.</strong> The simplest way to enjoy the uplands. Park at an appropriate lakeside visitor area, look across toward Haruna-Fuji and take a short walk. Allow <strong>45–75 minutes</strong>. Maps: <strong>榛名湖</strong>. This is the priority if you mainly want the scenery and the drive. <a href=\"https://www.visit-gunma.jp/en/spots/lake-haruna/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Lake guide</a></li><li><strong>Haruna-Fuji Ropeway.</strong> A way to get a higher view without making a long climb. Allow <strong>45–75 minutes</strong>, with extra time for a queue if necessary. Maps: <strong>榛名山ロープウェイ</strong>. Choose it if it is operating and visibility makes the ride worthwhile. There is a small shrine at the summit. <a href=\"https://www.visit-gunma.jp/en/discover/stories-and-guides/36-hours-ikaho/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Gunma&#x27;s description of the lake and ropeway outing</a></li><li><strong>Ikaho&#x27;s stone steps and shrine.</strong> For a town walk, street snacks and the hillside setting. Allow <strong>60–90 minutes</strong> if climbing the main steps; a shorter look is possible. Maps: <strong>伊香保温泉石段街</strong>. This visit works perfectly well without a bath. <a href=\"https://www.visit-gunma.jp/en/discover/destinations/ikaho/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Ikaho visitor guide</a></li></ol>","A manageable visit":"<p>Leave Hashimoto in the morning. Choose <strong>the lake plus either the ropeway or the steps</strong>. Keep a comfortable margin for the descent, fuel and the rental handover. An <strong>arrival target around 5 p.m. in Honjo</strong> would leave useful room before the booked 6:30 p.m. return; that is a planning target, not an additional booking requirement. Set the mountain departure time using that day&#x27;s navigation estimate.</p><p>The unhurried version is entirely respectable: enjoy the climb, stop by the lake, have lunch and bring the car back. Haruna Shrine, farther around the mountain, would be a separate addition; it is not part of this page&#x27;s three-stop menu.</p>","YOKAI ALERT":"<p><strong>Coverage:</strong> Hashimoto → Ikaho → the Haruna uplands, lake / ropeway options, and descent toward the Honjo rental return.</p><p><strong>Primary entities: tengu, yamabiko and lake-associated kappa.</strong></p><p><strong>At the stop:</strong> Ikaho&#x27;s stair routes provide repeated opportunities for a call to be heard from above the speaker. The Haruna shoreline adds water-edge exposure. A reflection that continues ascending after its owner stops is not a reliable guide to the ropeway queue.</p><p><strong>On the route:</strong> yamabiko interference can repeat a turn instruction beyond the turn itself. Tengu may propose a shorter descent requiring the vehicle to leave the road entirely. Neither instruction is to be accepted.</p><p><strong>Procedure:</strong> follow the selected road and public visitor paths. Any investigation must wait until the car is parked normally. Do not approach the lake in response to a request for assistance from one&#x27;s own reflection.</p>","NINJA WARNING REPORT":"<p><strong>Risk profile: hairpin observation, stairway interception and delayed departure.</strong> Tree cover and bends provide intermittent visibility on the approach. At Ikaho, a message can be passed down several flights of steps without its sender being seen. At Haruna, the principal exposure is an invitation to inspect one further viewpoint.</p><p>Retain the planned descent time. Smoke, banners and rooftop signals do not authorise a pursuit or roadside stop. No ninja appointment is to be accepted that requires attendance after the party should have departed for Honjo.</p>","SPECIAL CONCERNS":"<ul><li><strong>Deadline displacement:</strong> the car is due in Honjo by 6:30 p.m. on 22 October. Apparent changes in the sun&#x27;s position do not amend the booking.</li><li><strong>Final-stop proliferation:</strong> a tanuki may describe each additional viewpoint as being on the way. Require it to appear on the actual route before consideration.</li><li><strong>Return documentation:</strong> a leaf bearing a rental-company logo is not an extension agreement. Fuel and handover time remain part of the day&#x27;s schedule.</li></ul>"}},{"id":"ako","title":"Ako","ja":"赤穂","region":"HYOGO","label":"Retained coastal alternative","day":17,"end":18,"color":"#086868","accent":"#ffe1a0","crop":"86% 77%","hotel":"Shiosai Kirara Shokichi","address":"2-8 Misaki, Ako, Hyogo 678-0215","checkin":"15:00","checkout":"10:00","route":"Circuit → Ako → Osaka · only if this alternative is chosen","drive":"Alternative route · driving time to be refreshed","type":"alternative","notice":"STILL BOOKED. Retained pending discussion with your travelling companion. Monterey Himeji is also booked for 17–18 October.","sections":{"The area":"<p>This section is retained for the still-active Ako reservation. The hotel is in the <strong>Misaki coastal area</strong>, while the castle and much of the town&#x27;s historical interpretation are inland. The historic port of <strong>Sakoshi</strong> is another separate area.</p><p>Its appeal is the coastal setting. The user currently prefers Himeji for a more substantial town walk; keeping this section does not mean Ako has been restored as the intended overnight.</p>","Why it matters":"<p>Ako is closely associated with the <strong>forty-seven ronin</strong> and the revenge story later celebrated as <strong>Chushingura</strong>. The history concerns former retainers avenging their lord; subsequent theatre and retellings helped turn it into one of Japan&#x27;s best-known stories about loyalty. The castle site and Oishi Shrine give the story a geographical home. It is worth keeping the documented events and their dramatic afterlife distinct. <a href=\"https://www.japan.travel/en/spot/2351/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">JNTO&#x27;s Ako Castle guide</a>, <a href=\"https://ako-kankou.jp/en/sightseeing/ako-ooishijinjya.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Oishi Shrine&#x27;s local tourism entry</a></p><p>Salt supplies the less theatrical but equally important history. Ako developed into a major producer, with extensive salt fields supporting the local economy. This helps explain the town&#x27;s links to maritime trade and the coast&#x27;s former working character. What now looks like a leisurely seaside escape was also an industrial landscape. <a href=\"https://www.hyogo-tourism.jp/world/review/detail_230.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Hyogo&#x27;s salt history</a></p>","Three picks":"<ol><li><strong>Ako Castle ruins and Oishi Shrine.</strong> Treat these as one historical outing. The walls, moat, grounds and shrine provide the strongest introduction to the town&#x27;s samurai connections. Allow <strong>75–120 minutes</strong>. Maps: <strong>赤穂城跡</strong> and <strong>赤穂大石神社</strong>. Expect a castle site and reconstructed elements, rather than an intact original main keep. <a href=\"https://ako-castle.jp/en/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Ako Castle Digital Museum</a></li><li><strong>Misaki coast, Iwatsuhime Shrine and Kirakira Slope.</strong> A short coastal walk with sea views, steps and places to stop for something to eat or drink. Allow <strong>45–75 minutes</strong>, or longer if you settle into a café. Maps: <strong>伊和都比売神社</strong> and <strong>きらきら坂</strong>. This is the natural choice around your coastal stay. <a href=\"https://www.hyogo-tourism.jp/world/spot/detail_1087.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Hyogo&#x27;s Kirakira Slope guide</a></li><li><strong>Sakoshi&#x27;s old port streets.</strong> For a quieter walk through a former trading port, with traditional buildings and a waterfront setting. Allow <strong>45–75 minutes</strong>. Maps: <strong>坂越の町並み</strong>. It makes sense as an alternative to a longer castle visit, rather than a mandatory extra circuit of town. <a href=\"https://osaka-info.jp/en/information/around-trip/kansai/course06/day3.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Regional itinerary covering Sakoshi</a></li></ol>","A manageable visit":"<p>If Ako is chosen after discussion, a coastal walk after the circuit and the castle or Sakoshi the following morning would use the stay well. Recheck that alternative route before using it. If your hotel rate includes dinner, use its actual meal time to set Saturday&#x27;s arrival target; the screenshots alone do not establish your meal plan.</p>","YOKAI ALERT":"<p><strong>Coverage:</strong> The retained Ako alternative: circuit → Shiosai Kirara Shokichi, the Misaki coast, optional castle / Sakoshi visits, and onward travel to Osaka.</p><p><strong>Primary entities: funayurei, umibozu and bake-danuki.</strong></p><p><strong>At the stop:</strong> shoreline lights may imply an occupied vessel where no hull is visible. A dark form rising beyond the harbour should not be approached for a clearer photograph. Bake-danuki interference concerns meal arrangements, room identity and attempts to exchange paid accommodation for a view from a tree.</p><p><strong>On the route:</strong> coastal turn-offs can lead away from the actual hotel while retaining a convincing sea view. The sea&#x27;s continued presence does not establish correct navigation.</p><p><strong>Procedure:</strong> if this option is chosen, use the full Shokichi hotel name. Stay within public coastal paths and do not follow calls from beyond the waterline. Any additional room occupant must be checked against the reservation.</p>","NINJA WARNING REPORT":"<p><strong>Risk profile: coastal lookout positions and historical identity confusion.</strong> Headlands provide observation of approaching traffic; old port streets provide concealed pedestrian connections. A purported courier may attempt to attach an unresolved historical grievance to the onward Osaka journey.</p><p>Do not carry sealed revenge instructions, accept a substitute destination, or investigate signals from private waterfront premises. Keep the party&#x27;s departure decision independent of any assembly of armed retainers outside the visitor area.</p>","SPECIAL CONCERNS":"<ul><li><strong>Booking status:</strong> this reservation remains active pending discussion with the travelling companion. Himeji is the intended stop; Ako remains an alternative.</li><li><strong>Salt claims:</strong> coastal spirits may demand compensation for corrosion originating before the vehicle was manufactured. Refer to the rental inspection record.</li><li><strong>Meal-time authority:</strong> use the actual hotel meal arrangement if applicable. A dinner bell heard only from offshore does not establish a seating time.</li></ul>"}}],"outside":[{"title":"Tokyo · Tabata","hotel":"Tokyo City View Hotel Tabata-Station","day":9,"end":13,"address":"1-24-22 Tabata, Kita-ku, Tokyo 114-0014","notice":"Before the driving loop."},{"title":"Yokohama","hotel":"InterContinental Yokohama Grand","day":22,"end":23,"address":"1-1-1 Minatomirai, Nishi-ku, Yokohama, Kanagawa 220-8522","notice":"Intentional parallel booking with ANA InterContinental Tokyo on 22 October."},{"title":"Tokyo · Akasaka","hotel":"ANA InterContinental Tokyo","day":22,"end":23,"address":"1-12-33 Akasaka, Minato-ku, Tokyo 107-0052","notice":"Intentional parallel booking with Yokohama on 22 October."},{"title":"Tokyo · Akasaka","hotel":"ANA InterContinental Tokyo","day":23,"end":25,"address":"1-12-33 Akasaka, Minato-ku, Tokyo 107-0052","notice":"Contiguous with the following booking."},{"title":"Tokyo · Akasaka","hotel":"ANA InterContinental Tokyo","day":25,"end":27,"address":"1-12-33 Akasaka, Minato-ku, Tokyo 107-0052","notice":"Final checkout: Tuesday 27 October."}],"returnCar":{"name":"Toyota Rent a Car Honjowaseda Station Shop","address":"1-9-41 Wasedanomori, Honjo, Saitama 367-0030","day":22,"time":"18:30","phone":"0495-24-3100"},"revision":"October 2026 · current booking corrections included"};
